A realtime computation past max_rt_quantum is held to it rather than refused, when the constraint allows it (a
computation below half the constraint is already raised). The board's timer runs at 19.2 MHz: a client that computes its
policy in another timebase's ticks -- coreaudiod asks 1200000 (50 ms at 24 MHz) -- was refused, every I/O thread stayed
non-realtime and the HAL's clock never moved. With the 24 MHz timebase userspace sees (droidvm-timebase-24mhz) the request
fits; this keeps a client that still overshoots realtime. From macOS-DroidVM patches/xnu-12377/0039 (lateautumn233).

diff --git a/osfmk/kern/thread_policy.c b/osfmk/kern/thread_policy.c
index bb1f51b..cf36182 100644
--- a/osfmk/kern/thread_policy.c
+++ b/osfmk/kern/thread_policy.c
@@ -405,6 +405,15 @@ thread_policy_set_internal(
 		info = (thread_time_constraint_with_priority_policy_t)policy_info;
 
 
+		/*
+		 * A computation past the maximum is held to it rather than refused: the board's timer runs at 19.2 MHz,
+		 * where 50 ms is 960000 ticks, and coreaudiod asks for its I/O thread a computation of 1200000 ticks (50 ms
+		 * at Apple's 24 MHz), so every I/O thread it starts stays non-realtime and the HAL's clock never moves.
+		 */
+		if (info->computation > max_rt_quantum && info->constraint >= max_rt_quantum) {
+			info->computation = max_rt_quantum;
+		}
+
 		if (info->constraint < info->computation ||
 		    info->computation > max_rt_quantum ||
 		    info->computation < min_rt_quantum) {
